document.getElementById("p-grammar").innerHTML = "<style>\n#p-grammar p, #p-grammar li { max-width: 72ch; }\n#p-grammar .tw { overflow-x: auto; }\n#p-grammar .gr-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(400px, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin: 14px 0 6px; }\n#p-grammar .gr-card { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px 12px 10px; min-width: 0; }\n#p-grammar .gr-card h4 { margin: 0 0 4px; font-size: 0.95rem; }\n#p-grammar .gr-badge { display: inline-block; margin: 0 0 6px; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 0.76rem; font-weight: 600; }\n#p-grammar .gr-badge.real { background: var(--accent-soft); color: var(--accent); }\n#p-grammar .gr-badge.emu { background: var(--warn-soft); color: var(--warn); }\n#p-grammar .gr-cell { margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--muted); }\n#p-grammar .gr-cell code { font-size: 0.9em; }\n#p-grammar .gr-note { margin: 6px 0 0; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--muted); }\n#p-grammar .gr-card img { width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; background: #fff; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; }\n#p-grammar .gr-card pre { margin: 8px 0 6px; font-size: 0.74rem; overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; }\n#p-grammar pre.gr-marks { font-size: 0.74rem; overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; }\n#p-grammar details { margin: 6px 0; }\n#p-grammar details summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }\n#p-grammar details pre { font-size: 0.76rem; overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; }\n#p-grammar figure { margin: 0; }\n#p-grammar figcaption { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); max-width: 72ch; margin: 6px 0 22px; }\n#p-grammar figcaption p { margin: 0 0 8px; }\n</style>\n\n<section class=\"lede\">\n  <p>This tab puts ggdist and ggdibbler inside GoFish. The claim is that neither package needs a grammar of its own: once draws are rows, every chart in either package is a GoFish flow over those rows. GoFish needs four additions, listed in section 1. This is version 2 of the claim. Version 1 was checked against every example in both packages (the <a href=\"#catalog\">Catalog</a> tab: 96 ggdist vignette charts and 122 ggdibbler examples), and section 4 lists what that check changed. Section 5 compares real ggdist and ggdibbler renders with the GoFish spec for each.</p>\n</section>\n\n<h2>1. What GoFish needs</h2>\n<p>A cell is one uncertain value, such as one bar's height or one map region's rate.</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong><code>sample(data, { n })</code></strong> is a data step. It turns each distribution cell into n rows and adds two columns: <code>draw</code>, which says which draw a row is, and <code>level</code>, the row's quantile level inside its own cell (its value's CDF, a number from 0 to 1). <code>n</code> may be <code>Infinity</code>. <code>at: \"quantiles\"</code> takes evenly spaced levels instead of random draws, which is what ggdist's dotplots of a distribution do. Because it is a data step, several layers can read one sample. Data that already arrives as draws declares its draw key in the schema, as in the proposal, and gets its <code>level</code> from each cell's ranks.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>blend: \"mean\"</code></strong> on <code>group</code> puts every draw in one frame and averages them. This is from <a href=\"#limits\">Blur as a limit</a>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Summaries of the draws.</strong> A summary takes the list of draws in a group and returns one value, such as the mean or a quantile, or one curve, such as the density. The summaries are <code>mean</code>, <code>median</code>, <code>quantile(p)</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, the probability of a bin, and the density curve. In a channel they are methods, such as <code>value.median()</code>. Inside a <code>derive</code>, two data helpers turn them into rows: <code>densityCurve</code> gives the density curve as rows, and <code>centralIntervals</code> gives one row per interval level. Some summaries exist today. They are not special to draws, but ggdist is built on them.</li>\n  <li><strong>The draw key's type.</strong> The key records whether draws are shared across cells (draw 3 is one whole run of a model) or made per cell, and whether n is finite. Section 3 gives the rules that read it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Everything else is GoFish as it is today.</p>\n<!-- marks -->\n<h3>Marks defined in GoFish</h3>\n<p>ggdist's slab, interval and line ribbon are not new built-in marks. They are ordinary user code, written below in today's GoFish, and every spec in this tab and in the Catalog uses them. Each one is a function that returns a mark. The interval returns a function of the group's rows, as <code>.mark((rows) =&gt; ...)</code> already allows, because each of its levels gets its own fixed thickness. <code>ccdf</code> is the slab with thickness P(value &gt; v) in place of the density. <code>gradientinterval</code> is a variant of the slab that carries a GoFish gap.</p>\n<p>The definitions use two data helpers. The specs can be read without them.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>densityCurve(rows, x, { n, range, normalize })</code> gives the density curve of the draws as n rows <code>{ v, density, cdf }</code>. <code>range: [lo, hi]</code> sets where the curve starts and ends; an end left out keeps its default. <code>normalize: \"peak\"</code> divides the density by its peak. On a sample, <code>cdf</code> is the sample's ECDF.</li>\n  <li><code>centralIntervals(rows, x, levels, { by })</code> gives one row per level <code>{ level, center, size, median }</code>: the central interval that holds that share of the draws.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The core of each mark, without options:</p>\n<pre class=\"gr-marks\"><code class=\"language-js\">// slab: the density curve of the draws, drawn as a ribbon\nconst slab = (x) =&gt;\n  chart()\n    .flow(derive((rows) =&gt; densityCurve(rows, x)), scatter({ x: \"v\" }))\n    .mark(ribbon({ h: \"density\" }));\n\n// ccdf: like the slab, but its thickness is P(value &gt; v), the chance the value is above v\nconst ccdf = (x) =&gt;\n  chart()\n    .flow(derive((rows) =&gt; densityCurve(rows, x).map((r) =&gt; ({ ...r, ccdf: 1 - r.cdf }))),\n          scatter({ x: \"v\" }))\n    .mark(ribbon({ h: \"ccdf\" }));\n\n// interval: one rect per central interval (the wider, the thinner), and a circle at the median\nconst interval = (x, levels = [0.95, 0.66]) =&gt; (rows) =&gt; {\n  const ints = centralIntervals(rows, x, levels);\n  return layer([\n    ...ints.map((d, i) =&gt; rect({ cx: v(d.center), w: v(d.size), h: 1.5 + 2.5 * i })),\n    circle({ cx: v(ints[0].median), r: 4 }),\n  ]);\n};\n\n// lineribbon: at each value of along, a ribbon per central interval, and a line through the medians\nconst lineribbon = (y, { along, levels = [0.95, 0.8, 0.5] }) =&gt; layer([\n  chart().flow(derive((rows) =&gt; centralIntervals(rows, y, levels, { by: along })),\n               group({ by: \"level\" }), scatter({ by: along, x: along }))\n    .mark(rect({ w: 0, cy: \"center\", h: \"size\" })).layer(ribbon({ fill: \"level\" })),\n  chart().flow(derive((rows) =&gt; centralIntervals(rows, y, [0], { by: along })),\n               scatter({ by: along, x: along, y: \"median\" }))\n    .mark(line()),\n]);</code></pre>\n<details><summary>Full definitions, with the options the catalog uses (sides, direction, highest-density intervals, the gradient and CCDF variants)</summary>\n<pre class=\"gr-marks\"><code class=\"language-js\">// slab: the density curve of the draws, as a ribbon. side \"start\" hangs it below the line,\n// and \"both\" mirrors it. density holds densityCurve's options; curve(c, rows) may cut or\n// rewrite the curve's rows.\nconst slab = (x, { dir = \"x\", side = \"end\", density = {}, curve = (c) =&gt; c, ...o } = {}) =&gt; {\n  const half = (s) =&gt; chart()\n    .flow(derive((rows) =&gt; curve(densityCurve(rows, x, density), rows).map((r) =&gt; ({ ...r, density: s * r.density }))),\n          scatter({ [dir]: \"v\" }))\n    .mark(ribbon({ dir, [dir === \"x\" ? \"h\" : \"w\"]: \"density\", fill: SLAB, ...o }));\n  return side === \"both\" ? layer([half(1), half(-1)]) : half(side === \"start\" ? -1 : 1);\n};\n\n// interval: one rect per interval of the draws, the widest drawn first and thinnest, and a\n// circle at the median. A rect sits at its interval's center, the midpoint, which is the\n// median only when the distribution is symmetric (not a lognormal, a beta, or most samples).\nconst interval = (x, { levels = [0.95, 0.66], dir = \"x\", intervals = centralIntervals,\n                       point = \"median\", fill = INK, ...o } = {}) =&gt; (rows) =&gt; {\n  const [c, s, t] = dir === \"x\" ? [\"cx\", \"w\", \"h\"] : [\"cy\", \"h\", \"w\"];\n  const ints = intervals(rows, x, levels), n = ints.length;\n  return layer([\n    ...ints.map((d, i) =&gt; rect({ [c]: v(d.center), [s]: v(d.size),\n                                 [t]: n === 1 ? 4 : 1.5 + (2.5 * i) / (n - 1), fill, ...o })),\n    ...(point ? [circle({ [c]: v(ints[0][point]), r: 4, fill })] : []),\n  ]);\n};\n\n// lineribbon: at each value of along, one ribbon per central interval of the draws, and a\n// line through their medians.\nconst lineribbon = (y, { along, levels = [0.95, 0.8, 0.5], fill = \"level\", stroke = INK, ...o } = {}) =&gt; layer([\n  chart().flow(derive((rows) =&gt; centralIntervals(rows, y, levels, { by: along })),\n               group({ by: \"level\" }), scatter({ by: along, x: along }))\n    .mark(rect({ w: 0, cy: \"center\", h: \"size\" })).layer(ribbon({ fill, ...o })),\n  chart().flow(derive((rows) =&gt; centralIntervals(rows, y, [0], { by: along })),\n               scatter({ by: along, x: along, y: \"median\" }))\n    .mark(line({ stroke, strokeWidth: 2 })),\n]);\n\n// gradientinterval: the slab divided by its peak, read as opacity over the full height.\n// gap: GoFish has no opacity that varies along one region.\nconst gradientinterval = (x, o = {}) =&gt; slab(x, { density: { normalize: \"peak\" }, opacity: \"alpha\", ...o,\n  curve: (c) =&gt; c.map((r) =&gt; ({ ...r, alpha: r.density, density: 1 })) });\n\n// ccdf: the slab with thickness P(value &gt; v), the chance the value is above v, instead of the\n// density. ggdist starts the bar at the axis's lower limit: pass it as density: { range: [lo] }.\nconst ccdf = (x, o = {}) =&gt;\n  slab(x, { ...o, curve: (c) =&gt; c.map((r) =&gt; ({ ...r, density: 1 - r.cdf })) });</code></pre>\n</details>\n<!-- /marks -->\n\n<h2>2. Every chart is one way of using up the draws</h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n  <thead><tr><th>GoFish step that uses up the draws</th><th>At finite n</th><th>At n = Infinity</th><th>Package</th></tr></thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td><code>group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" })</code></td><td>overlay, averaged</td><td>the expected picture, e.g. a CCDF fade</td><td>ggdist CCDF fade (limit); no ggdibbler example averages</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>group({ by: \"draw\" })</code>, no blend</td><td>overlay, painted over each other</td><td>error: saturates, no limit</td><td>every ggdibbler overlay (fixed alpha); ggdist spaghetti</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>spread</code>, <code>time.sequence</code> or <code>table</code> by <code>\"draw\"</code></td><td>dodged bars, subdivided map, HOP, lineup</td><td>error: no limit</td><td>ggdibbler</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>spread({ by: \"level\" })</code></td><td>sorted dodged bars, sorted subdivided map</td><td>error: infinitely many bars of zero width. As n grows the sorted bars tend to the CCDF bar, which <code>ccdf</code> draws as a summary</td><td>ggdist CCDF bar (as <code>ccdf</code>)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td><code>partition</code> by a bin of the value, then <code>stack</code></td><td>dotplot</td><td>error: use the density curve of the draws</td><td>ggdist dots</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>a summary of each cell's draws</td><td>empirical mean, interval, median line</td><td>exact quantiles, intervals, density slab</td><td>ggdist interval, point, slab, lineribbon</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>a summary per draw, then steps on the draws</td><td>rank whole curves by depth, keep the deepest</td><td>curvewise bands</td><td>ggdist <code>curve_interval</code></td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table></div>\n\n<h2>3. Rules the draw key's type checks</h2>\n<h3>3.1 n = Infinity</h3>\n<p>An infinite draw key has no list of draws to place. A chart may use one only if every step that reads it turns the draws into something with a meaning at the limit: an average picture (<code>blend: \"mean\"</code>) or a summary of the draws. A step that places one mark per draw is an error at n = Infinity, because infinitely many marks cannot be drawn. That covers <code>spread</code>, <code>time.sequence</code> or <code>table</code> by <code>draw</code>, painting without a blend, and <code>stack</code> in a bin. It also covers a <code>spread</code> by <code>level</code>, which places one mark per row in order of level: at n = Infinity each mark would have zero width. A channel that reads <code>draw</code> (such as color by draw), or a draw key that nothing uses up, is an error too.</p>\n<p>Sorted bars do have a limit. As n grows, the bars of a <code>spread</code> by <code>level</code> tend to the CCDF bar, which the <code>ccdf</code> mark of section 1 draws from a summary of the draws. That is a relation between two specs, one at finite n and one that summarizes the draws. It is not one spec at n = Infinity. <code>level</code> itself stays legal at n = Infinity when a summary or a channel reads it, such as an opacity from <code>level</code>, and nothing places a mark per row.</p>\n\n<h3>3.2 Combining cells within one draw</h3>\n<p>Many steps combine several cells into one shape: a line across x, a polygon, a scatter of two Dist columns, or a stat computed per draw over many cells (density, contour, smoother, boxplot). The shape then shows one joint value of those cells, and which joint it shows depends on the key that ties the cells together:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>a <strong>shared draw key</strong> shows the joint the data states. No message.</li>\n  <li>a <strong>per-cell draw key</strong> shows the cells as independent. That is a possible world only if the cells really are independent, which GoFish cannot know, so it gets a warning that the chart assumes independence.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>level</code></strong> shows the cells as moving in lockstep: every cell at its own 10th percentile at once. The data never states that, so it gets a warning that the shape is a quantile curve, not a draw. This also covers <code>at: \"quantiles\"</code> over several cells, which is how the ggdibbler paper's anti-example in Figure 6c can be written.</li>\n  <li>a <strong>summary per cell</strong>, such as a line through the median of each cell's draws, is not a draw. No message.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This makes precise the check discussed with Harriet in the <a href=\"#ggdibbler\">ggdibbler side by side</a> tab.</p>\n\n<h3>3.3 Stats that pool draws</h3>\n<p>A stat whose group holds draws of several cells from several draws, such as one smoother fitted to all drawn rows, treats n draws as n times as much data. It gets a warning. The fix is to fit once per draw and then use up the draw key. This is the pooled smooth in Figure 7a of the ggdibbler paper.</p>\n\n<h3>3.4 Stacking draws</h3>\n<p>Two draws of one cell are two alternatives, not two parts of a sum, so a <code>stack</code> along the draw key adds up alternatives. Both stacked-draw charts in the ggdibbler catalog do this, which is the paper's own argument. The stacked quantity there is a count, and a count can be summed, so the <code>CanSum</code> rule does not catch it. The draw key's type has to: a stack whose children differ by <code>draw</code> is an error. A dotplot is different. Its stack runs along a bin of one cell, each dot standing for probability 1/n, and probabilities of different draws do add.</p>\n\n<h2>4. What the catalog changed</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong><code>sample</code> moved from the flow to the data.</strong> Eight ggdibbler examples use one seed so two layers read the same draws. A step inside one chart's flow can't be shared.</li>\n  <li><strong><code>level</code> replaced sorting the draw key.</strong> Version 1 ranked draws with <code>field(\"draw\").sort(\"value\")</code>. That ranks inside a cell only because of how today's sort happens to work, and it breaks when a cell has several rows per draw. A <code>level</code> column says what it means. It also makes the line rule in 3.2 work: version 1's rule fired on ggdist's own median line.</li>\n  <li><strong>Summaries are a way to use up draws.</strong> About 60 ggdist charts draw an interval, and none could be written by filtering ranks. They need the quantiles of the draws.</li>\n  <li><strong>The slab is the density curve of the draws, not the limit of the dotplot.</strong> A dotplot reaches the slab only as the number of draws grows and the bin width shrinks together, so no single flow at n = Infinity gives it. This keeps Josh's 11-04 decision that the slab is separate. It is not a new built-in mark either. The <code>slab</code> of section 1 is the density curve in a <code>derive</code>, a <code>scatter</code> and a <code>ribbon</code>, and the <code>interval</code> is one <code>rect</code> per level and a <code>circle</code> at the median.</li>\n  <li><strong>ggdist has a second kind of mark.</strong> Its gradient interval sets opacity from the density scaled so the peak is opaque, and the Correll and Gleicher fades are flat in the middle and fade in the tails. Neither is the limit of any picture of draws. Each is a function of the distribution, drawn with an ordinary channel: the density curve or the CDF of the draws, then opacity from data. So <code>blend</code> stays a plain average with no rescaling option. Today GoFish lacks an opacity that varies along one region, which the catalog counts as a GoFish gap.</li>\n  <li><strong>M(U(X)) and U(M(X)) are kinds of charts, not packages.</strong> A chart that reads only <code>level</code> or summaries per cell depends only on each cell's own distribution. A chart that joins cells along a shared draw key, or summarizes each draw, depends on how cells move together. ggdist's <code>curve_interval</code> is the second kind. Every ggdibbler example uses per-cell draws, so in information they are all the first kind, even when they draw a line along the draw key (which 3.2 warns about).</li>\n  <li><strong>Two levels need a second source of draws.</strong> ggdist's MCSE dots show the uncertainty of a sample itself. That needs a <code>Dist&lt;Dist&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> made from a sample by resampling, and a way to name the second draw key, since <code>sample</code> always writes <code>draw</code> and <code>level</code>. Both are still missing, so the catalog counts these charts as not fitting.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Still open in version 2</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>The density curve and the CDF of the draws are curves, not single values. The catalog turns them into rows with <code>densityCurve</code>, so a channel reads a column. The text does not yet say whether a channel can read a curve directly.</li>\n  <li>What <code>sample</code> does on a cell that is not a distribution, and what a cell is for data that arrives as draws.</li>\n  <li>The fixed column names <code>draw</code> and <code>level</code> clash with data (LakeHuron has its own <code>level</code>) and with a second draw key.</li>\n  <li>Thinning a sample to n quantiles, for ggdist's quantile dots of a sample.</li>\n  <li>Band depth compares pairs of whole draws, so it is not a summary per draw (ggdist catalog entry 88).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>5. Charts side by side</h2>\n<p>Each card shows one chart. The badge says where its image comes from: a real ggdist or ggdibbler render, or a ggplot2 emulation of a GoFish flow that neither package makes. The line under the badge names the GoFish step that uses up the draws. Under the image is the proposed GoFish spec, then the R code that drew the image. All images use one seed (1980), one fill color and one theme. The script is <code>grammar/render.R</code>, and every R block assumes this setup:</p>\n<details><summary>R setup shared by all images</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">library(ggplot2)\nlibrary(distributional)\nlibrary(dplyr)\nlibrary(sf)\nlibrary(ggdist)\nlibrary(ggdibbler)\n\ndata_dir &lt;- \"proposals/uncertainty-specs/ggdibbler-r/data\"\nimg_dir &lt;- \"proposals/uncertainty-specs/grammar/img\"\ndir.create(img_dir, showWarnings = FALSE)\nFILL &lt;- \"#3a6ea5\"\ntheme_set(theme_minimal(base_size = 11) +\n  theme(panel.grid.minor = element_blank()))\nsave &lt;- function(plot, name, width = 4.2, height = 3) {\n  ggsave(file.path(img_dir, paste0(\"gr-\", name, \".png\")), plot,\n    width = width, height = height, dpi = 150, bg = \"white\"\n  )\n}\n\nbars &lt;- read.csv(file.path(data_dir, \"bars.csv\"))\ntiles &lt;- read.csv(file.path(data_dir, \"tiles.csv\"))\nbar_y &lt;- list(\n  expand_limits(y = 0),\n  coord_cartesian(ylim = c(0, 115), expand = c(FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, TRUE)),\n  labs(y = \"value\")\n)</code></pre></details>\n<p>The specs use today's GoFish plus the four additions of section 1. In them, <code>value</code> is a Dist expression. On a drawn row it reads that row's draw. With no <code>sample</code>, a summary of it, such as <code>value.median()</code>, is exact. <code>slab</code>, <code>interval</code> and <code>lineribbon</code> are the marks defined in section 1 in ordinary GoFish. <code>level</code> is the column <code>sample</code> adds. Cards that cannot be written this way say so.</p>\n\n<h3>5.1 One quantity, two channels</h3>\n<p>Five bars from <code>bars.csv</code>, each with a normal height: A is normal(28, 4), B is normal(55, 6), C is normal(43, 9), D is normal(91, 5) and E is normal(81, 12). Card a is ggdibbler's dodged bar chart. Card b is ggdibbler's overlay at 50 draws, and card c is the blend at the limit. Cards d and e place each bar's draws in order of level, at 20 draws and at the limit.</p>\n\n<figure>\n<div class=\"gr-grid\">\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>a. Dodged draws</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdibbler, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>spread({ by: \"draw\" })</code> · n = 20</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-bars-dodge.png\" alt=\"Five groups of 20 thin bars each, at uneven heights.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"se\" });\nchart(sample(bars, { n: 20 }))\n  .flow(\n    spread({ by: \"category\", dir: \"x\" }),\n    spread({ by: \"draw\", dir: \"x\", spacing: 0 }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ h: value }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">set.seed(1980)\np &lt;- bars |&gt;\n  mutate(value = dist_normal(mean, se)) |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(x = category, y = value)) +\n  geom_col_sample(times = 20, fill = FILL) +\n  bar_y\nsave(p, \"bars-dodge\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>b. Overlaid draws</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdibbler, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"draw\" })</code>, the plain overlay at opacity 0.05 · n = 50</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-bars-overlay.png\" alt=\"Five bars whose tops fade in steps.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"se\" });\nchart(sample(bars, { n: 50 }))\n  .flow(\n    spread({ by: \"category\", dir: \"x\" }),\n    group({ by: \"draw\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ h: value, opacity: 0.05 }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">set.seed(1980)\np &lt;- bars |&gt;\n  mutate(value = dist_normal(mean, se)) |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(x = category, y = value)) +\n  geom_col_sample(\n    times = 50, fill = FILL, alpha = 0.05,\n    position = \"stack_identity\"\n  ) +\n  bar_y\nsave(p, \"bars-overlay\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>c. CCDF fade</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdist, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" })</code> · n = Infinity</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-bars-fade.png\" alt=\"Five full-width bars whose tops fade smoothly.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"se\" });\nchart(sample(bars, { n: Infinity }))\n  .flow(\n    spread({ by: \"category\", dir: \"x\" }),\n    group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ h: value }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- bars |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(x = category, ydist = dist_normal(mean, se))) +\n  stat_slab(\n    aes(thickness = after_stat(1), slab_alpha = after_stat(1 - cdf)),\n    side = \"both\", scale = 0.9, fill_type = \"gradient\", fill = FILL\n  ) +\n  scale_slab_alpha_continuous(range = c(0, 1), limits = c(0, 1), guide = \"none\") +\n  bar_y\nsave(p, \"bars-fade\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>d. Dodged draws in order of level</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>spread({ by: \"level\" })</code> · n = 20</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-bars-sorted.png\" alt=\"Five groups of 20 thin bars, each group rising from left to right.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"se\" });\nchart(sample(bars, { n: 20 }))\n  .flow(\n    spread({ by: \"category\", dir: \"x\" }),\n    spread({ by: \"level\", dir: \"x\", spacing: 0 }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ h: value }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">set.seed(1980)\ndraws &lt;- bars |&gt;\n  slice(rep(1:n(), each = 20)) |&gt;\n  mutate(value = rnorm(n(), mean, se)) |&gt;\n  group_by(category) |&gt;\n  mutate(rank = rank(value)) |&gt;\n  ungroup()\np &lt;- ggplot(draws, aes(x = category, y = value, group = rank)) +\n  geom_col(position = position_dodge(width = 0.9), width = 0.9, fill = FILL) +\n  bar_y\nsave(p, \"bars-sorted\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>e. CCDF bar</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdist, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: the CDF of each bar's draws, as thickness 1 − cdf · exact, no <code>sample</code></p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-bars-ccdf.png\" alt=\"Five bars whose right edge rises in a smooth S curve.\">\n  <p class=\"gr-note\"><code>ccdf</code> is the variant of the slab in section 1 whose thickness is P(H &gt; y). The bar starts at the axis's lower limit, 0, as ggdist's does.</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"se\" });\nchart(bars)\n  .flow(spread({ by: \"category\", dir: \"x\" }))\n  .mark(ccdf(value, { dir: \"y\", density: { range: [0] } }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- bars |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(x = category, ydist = dist_normal(mean, se))) +\n  stat_ccdfinterval(\n    scale = 0.9, fill = FILL, show_interval = FALSE, show_point = FALSE\n  ) +\n  bar_y\nsave(p, \"bars-ccdf\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n</div>\n<figcaption><p>All five cards show one quantity, P(H &gt; y), the chance that a bar reaches height y. A mean blend of card b's bars tends to card c, whose opacity at height y is P(H &gt; y). As n grows, card d's sorted bars tend to card e, whose width at height y is P(H &gt; y). Card d places the bars from low level to high, so its ink sits on the right, and ggdist's default CCDF bar puts it on the right too. So the blend turns the probability into opacity, and <code>level</code> turns it into width.</p>\n<p>Card e is not card d at n = Infinity. A <code>spread</code> by <code>level</code> places one bar per draw, and infinitely many bars of zero width cannot be drawn, so rule 3.1 makes it an error there, as it does a spread by <code>draw</code>. Card e is a second spec: the <code>ccdf</code> mark, a summary of the draws. \"Card d tends to card e\" relates the two specs.</p>\n<p>Card b is the plain overlay, not the blend. ggplot2 draws transparent bars with \"over\" compositing, so where k of the 50 bars overlap the opacity is 1 − 0.95<sup>k</sup>, not k / 50. Its bottom is a little lighter than c's, and its fade bends differently. At n = Infinity the plain overlay saturates, which rule 3.1 makes an error. A mean blend would match c.</p></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>5.2 Dots, slab and interval</h3>\n<p>Three groups, each a normal: a is normal(5, 1), b is normal(7, 1.5) and c is normal(8.5, 0.7). ggdist's quantile dotplot takes n evenly spaced quantiles, bins them by value and stacks each bin. Its slab is the density, and its interval is a few quantiles. ggdibbler overlays the draws at low opacity.</p>\n<details><summary>R setup for this section</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">groups &lt;- data.frame(\n  group = c(\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"),\n  mean = c(5, 7, 8.5),\n  sd = c(1, 1.5, 0.7)\n)\ndots_x &lt;- list(coord_cartesian(xlim = c(1, 12), expand = c(TRUE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE)), labs(x = \"value\"))</code></pre></details>\n<figure>\n<div class=\"gr-grid\">\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>a. Quantile dotplot, 20 dots</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdist, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>partition</code> by a bin of the value, then <code>stack</code> · n = 20</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-dots-20.png\" alt=\"Three dotplots of 20 dots each.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(groups, { n: 20, at: \"quantiles\" }))\n  .flow(\n    spread({ by: \"group\", dir: \"y\" }),\n    partition({\n      by: value.bin(Bin.step(0.4)), // chosen by hand; ggdist picks the width so the tallest stack fits\n      dir: \"x\",\n    }),\n    stack({ dir: \"y\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(circle({ r: 4 }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- groups |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(y = group, xdist = dist_normal(mean, sd))) +\n  stat_dots(quantiles = 20, fill = FILL, color = NA) +\n  dots_x\nsave(p, \"dots-20\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>b. Quantile dotplot, 100 dots</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdist, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>partition</code> by a bin of the value, then <code>stack</code> · n = 100</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-dots-100.png\" alt=\"Three dotplots of 100 small dots each.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(groups, { n: 100, at: \"quantiles\" }))\n  .flow(\n    spread({ by: \"group\", dir: \"y\" }),\n    partition({\n      by: value.bin(Bin.step(0.2)), // chosen by hand; ggdist picks the width so the tallest stack fits\n      dir: \"x\",\n    }),\n    stack({ dir: \"y\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(circle({ r: 2 }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- groups |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(y = group, xdist = dist_normal(mean, sd))) +\n  stat_dots(quantiles = 100, fill = FILL, color = NA) +\n  dots_x\nsave(p, \"dots-100\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>c. Slab</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdist, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: the density curve of each cell's draws · exact, no <code>sample</code></p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-dots-slab.png\" alt=\"Three density curves, filled.\">\n  <p class=\"gr-note\"><code>slab</code> is the mark defined in section 1, in ordinary GoFish. It draws the density curve of the draws as a ribbon along x.</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(groups)\n  .flow(spread({ by: \"group\", dir: \"y\" }))\n  .mark(slab(value));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- groups |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(y = group, xdist = dist_normal(mean, sd))) +\n  stat_slab(fill = FILL) +\n  dots_x\nsave(p, \"dots-slab\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>d. Point and interval</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdist, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: the central intervals and the median of each cell's draws · exact, no <code>sample</code></p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-dots-interval.png\" alt=\"Three medians with thick 66% and thin 95% intervals.\">\n  <p class=\"gr-note\"><code>interval</code> is the mark defined in section 1. Each level is a <code>rect</code> placed by the center and size of its central interval, and the point is a <code>circle</code> at the median.</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(groups)\n  .flow(spread({ by: \"group\", dir: \"y\" }))\n  .mark(interval(value));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- groups |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(y = group, xdist = dist_normal(mean, sd))) +\n  stat_pointinterval(color = FILL, .width = c(0.66, 0.95)) +\n  dots_x\nsave(p, \"dots-interval\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>e. Overlaid draws</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdibbler, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"draw\" })</code>, the plain overlay at opacity 0.15 · n = 50</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-dots-overlay.png\" alt=\"Three rows of faint overlapping points.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(groups, { n: 50 }))\n  .flow(\n    spread({ by: \"group\", dir: \"y\" }),\n    group({ by: \"draw\" }),\n    scatter({ x: value }),\n  )\n  .mark(circle({ r: 3, opacity: 0.15 }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">set.seed(1980)\np &lt;- groups |&gt;\n  mutate(value = dist_normal(mean, sd)) |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(x = value, y = group)) +\n  geom_point_sample(times = 50, alpha = 0.15, color = FILL, size = 2.5) +\n  dots_x\nsave(p, \"dots-overlay\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n</div>\n<figcaption><p>Cards a and b are one flow at two values of n. With <code>at: \"quantiles\"</code>, <code>sample</code> takes the quantiles at n evenly spaced levels, which are the values ggdist's dotplot uses. ggdist then bins them with Wilkinson's method, which places the bins where the dots are. GoFish has only fixed bins, so its stacks would differ a little from ggdist's. That is a GoFish gap, not a draw question.</p>\n<p>Card c is not the limit of cards a and b. A dotplot reaches the slab only if the number of dots grows and the bin width shrinks together, so no flow at a single n = Infinity gives it, and rule 3.1 makes a stack in a bin an error there. The slab is the density curve of the draws. Card d is the quantiles and the median of the draws, which are exact on a Dist. Card e paints 50 draws over each other. With <code>blend: \"mean\"</code> in its place, the limit would be a band whose darkness is the density times the dot's width. That is not ggdist's gradient interval either, because ggdist scales the opacity so the densest point is opaque (section 4).</p></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>5.3 Joint versus marginal: forecast paths</h3>\n<p>Fifty shared forecast paths over h = 1 to 20. Each path is a random walk that starts at 50 and takes steps from normal(2, 2), so draw 3 at h = 5 and draw 3 at h = 6 are one path. Card a gives ggdibbler what it takes today: at each h, a normal with the mean and standard deviation of the 50 paths there. ggdibbler then draws each h on its own.</p>\n<details><summary>R setup for this section</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># 50 shared forecast paths: a random walk from 50 with steps ~ Normal(2, 2).\nset.seed(1980)\npaths &lt;- expand.grid(h = 1:20, draw = 1:50) |&gt;\n  group_by(draw) |&gt;\n  mutate(value = 50 + cumsum(rnorm(20, 2, 2))) |&gt;\n  ungroup()\nby_h &lt;- paths |&gt;\n  group_by(h) |&gt;\n  summarise(mean = mean(value), sd = sd(value))\npaths_y &lt;- coord_cartesian(ylim = c(40, 110))</code></pre></details>\n<figure>\n<div class=\"gr-grid\">\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>a. Lines through draws made per h</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdibbler, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"draw\" })</code>, the plain overlay · n = 50 · draws made per cell · rule 3.2 warns</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-paths-dibbler.png\" alt=\"Fifty jagged lines rising from left to right.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(byH, { n: 50 }))\n  .flow(\n    group({ by: \"draw\" }),\n    scatter({ by: \"h\", x: \"h\", y: value }),\n  )\n  .mark(line({ opacity: 0.25 }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">set.seed(1980)\np &lt;- by_h |&gt;\n  mutate(value = dist_normal(mean, sd)) |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(x = h, y = value)) +\n  geom_line_sample(times = 50, alpha = 0.25, color = FILL) +\n  paths_y\nsave(p, \"paths-dibbler\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>b. The shared paths</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"draw\" })</code>, the plain overlay · n = 50 · shared draws</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-paths-shared.png\" alt=\"Fifty smooth lines rising and fanning out.\">\n  <p class=\"gr-note\">ggdist users draw this with plain <code>geom_line</code> too. It is not a ggdist geom.</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">// paths: one row per draw and h. The schema declares\n// draw as a draw key shared across h.\nchart(paths, { schema: { value: { draws: \"draw\" } } })\n  .flow(\n    group({ by: \"draw\" }),\n    scatter({ by: \"h\", x: \"h\", y: \"value\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(line({ opacity: 0.25 }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- ggplot(paths, aes(x = h, y = value, group = draw)) +\n  geom_line(alpha = 0.25, color = FILL) +\n  paths_y\nsave(p, \"paths-shared\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>c. Line ribbon</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdist, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: the central intervals and the median of the draws at each h · n = 50</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-paths-ribbon.png\" alt=\"A median line inside 50%, 80% and 95% bands.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">chart(paths).mark(\n  lineribbon(\"value\", {\n    along: \"h\",\n    fill: \"#3a6ea5\",\n    opacity: 0.4,\n  }),\n);</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- ggplot(paths, aes(x = h, y = value)) +\n  stat_lineribbon(.width = c(0.5, 0.8, 0.95), color = \"#1f3b5c\") +\n  scale_fill_manual(values = c(\"#c6d4e6\", \"#94b0d2\", \"#5f88bb\")) +\n  paths_y +\n  theme(legend.position = \"inside\", legend.position.inside = c(0.15, 0.78))\nsave(p, \"paths-ribbon\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>d. Lines through one level at every h</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"level\" })</code> · n = 50 · joins cells along <code>level</code>: rule 3.2 warns</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-paths-rank.png\" alt=\"Fifty smooth lines that never cross, rising and fanning out.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">// level: each row's quantile level among the 50 draws\n// at its h, from the ranks.\nchart(paths, { schema: { value: { draws: \"draw\" } } })\n  .flow(\n    group({ by: \"level\" }),\n    scatter({ by: \"h\", x: \"h\", y: \"value\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(line({ opacity: 0.25 }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">ranked &lt;- paths |&gt;\n  group_by(h) |&gt;\n  mutate(rank = rank(value)) |&gt;\n  ungroup()\np &lt;- ggplot(ranked, aes(x = h, y = value, group = rank)) +\n  geom_line(alpha = 0.25, color = FILL) +\n  paths_y\nsave(p, \"paths-rank\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n</div>\n<figcaption><p>Cards b and d look alike: both are 50 lines through the same 50 values at each h. They mean different things. Each line in b is one path of the model. Each line in d goes through the k-th smallest value at every h, so it is a quantile curve, and its lines never cross. Card a joins draws made per h, so its lines zigzag more than any path in b. Card c keeps each h's own distribution and says nothing about paths, which is a fair choice. Card d looks like it says something about paths and does not.</p>\n<p>Rule 3.2 tells these apart from the spec alone. Card b joins cells along a shared draw key, so it gets no warning. Cards a and d join cells along a per-cell draw key and along <code>level</code>, so both get a warning. The median line in card c goes through the median of the draws at each h, which is a summary, so it gets no warning. Version 1 could not make this cut, because it spelled level as a sort of the draw key, and its rule fired on the median line too.</p></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>5.4 Maps</h3>\n<p>An 8 by 6 grid of square regions from <code>tiles.csv</code> stands in for a map. Each region holds a normal whose standard deviation grows to the east, from 2 to 23. All three cards use one palette, ColorBrewer Blues from 25 to 105.</p>\n<details><summary>R setup for this section</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">square &lt;- function(col, row) {\n  st_polygon(list(rbind(\n    c(col, -row), c(col + 1, -row), c(col + 1, -row - 1),\n    c(col, -row - 1), c(col, -row)\n  )))\n}\ntiles_sf &lt;- tiles |&gt;\n  mutate(geometry = st_sfc(mapply(square, col, row, SIMPLIFY = FALSE))) |&gt;\n  st_as_sf()\nmap_fill &lt;- scale_fill_distiller(\n  palette = \"Blues\", direction = 1, limits = c(25, 105),\n  oob = scales::squish, guide = \"none\"\n)\noutline &lt;- geom_sf(\n  data = tiles_sf, aes(geometry = geometry),\n  fill = NA, colour = \"white\", linewidth = 0.7\n)</code></pre></details>\n<figure>\n<div class=\"gr-grid\">\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>a. Subdivided</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdibbler, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>table({ by: \"draw\", numCols: 4 })</code> inside each region · n = 16</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-map-subdivide.png\" alt=\"A grid of regions, each split into 16 cells of mixed shades; the eastern regions are grainy.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(tiles, { n: 16 }), {\n  color: Color.gradient([\"#f7fbff\", \"#6baed6\", \"#08306b\"]),\n})\n  .flow(\n    table({ by: { x: \"col\", y: \"row\" }, spacing: 2 }),\n    table({ by: \"draw\", numCols: 4, spacing: 0 }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ fill: value }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">set.seed(1980)\np &lt;- tiles_sf |&gt;\n  mutate(value = dist_normal(mean, sd)) |&gt;\n  ggplot() +\n  geom_sf_sample(aes(geometry = geometry, fill = value),\n    times = 16, linewidth = 0\n  ) +\n  outline +\n  map_fill +\n  theme_void()\nsave(p, \"map-subdivide\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>b. The limit of the blend</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" })</code> · n = Infinity</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-map-blend.png\" alt=\"A grid of regions, each one flat shade.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(tiles, { n: Infinity }), {\n  color: Color.gradient([\"#f7fbff\", \"#6baed6\", \"#08306b\"]),\n})\n  .flow(\n    table({ by: { x: \"col\", y: \"row\" }, spacing: 2 }),\n    group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ fill: value }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># The limit of the blend: each region's color is the average (in sRGB) of\n# the colors of 4000 of its draws, through the same palette as the others.\nset.seed(1980)\navg_color &lt;- function(mean, sd) {\n  rgb_draws &lt;- col2rgb(map_fill$map(rnorm(4000, mean, sd)))\n  rgb(t(rowMeans(rgb_draws)), maxColorValue = 255)\n}\np &lt;- tiles_sf |&gt;\n  mutate(color = mapply(avg_color, mean, sd)) |&gt;\n  ggplot() +\n  geom_sf(aes(geometry = geometry, fill = I(color)), colour = NA) +\n  outline +\n  theme_void()\nsave(p, \"map-blend\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>c. Subdivided in order of level</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>table({ by: \"level\", numCols: 4 })</code> · n = 16</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-map-sorted.png\" alt=\"A grid of regions, each a 16-cell ramp from pale at the top left to dark at the bottom right.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(tiles, { n: 16 }), {\n  color: Color.gradient([\"#f7fbff\", \"#6baed6\", \"#08306b\"]),\n})\n  .flow(\n    table({ by: { x: \"col\", y: \"row\" }, spacing: 2 }),\n    table({ by: \"level\", numCols: 4, spacing: 0 }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ fill: value }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># 16 draws per region, sorted and laid into a 4 x 4 grid in reading order.\nset.seed(1980)\ncells &lt;- tiles |&gt;\n  slice(rep(1:n(), each = 16)) |&gt;\n  mutate(value = rnorm(n(), mean, sd)) |&gt;\n  group_by(col, row) |&gt;\n  arrange(value, .by_group = TRUE) |&gt;\n  mutate(i = row_number() - 1) |&gt;\n  ungroup() |&gt;\n  mutate(\n    x0 = col + (i %% 4) / 4,\n    y0 = -row - (i %/% 4 + 1) / 4\n  )\np &lt;- ggplot(cells) +\n  geom_rect(aes(\n    xmin = x0, xmax = x0 + 0.25, ymin = y0, ymax = y0 + 0.25,\n    fill = value\n  )) +\n  outline +\n  map_fill +\n  theme_void()\nsave(p, \"map-sorted\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n</div>\n<figcaption><p>Cards a and c carry the spread: the eastern regions hold pale and dark cells side by side. Card b has lost it. Blues is close to a straight palette, so the average color of a region's draws is close to the color of its mean, and a wide region looks like a narrow one with the same mean. Card c puts each region's 16 draws in order of level from the top left, so the spread shows as how far the ramp runs from pale to dark.</p></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>5.5 Heatmap</h3>\n<p>A heatmap of 6 by 4 tiles. Each tile holds a normal. The means rise to the east, from 30 to 90 in steps of 12. The spreads rise to the south: the standard deviation is 2, 6, 12 and 25 in the four rows. All four cards use one palette, ColorBrewer Blues from 30 to 90, with values outside that range drawn at its ends. Cards a and b use n = 16 draws per tile.</p>\n<details><summary>R setup for this section</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># A 6 x 4 heatmap. Each tile is a normal. The means rise to the east from 30\n# to 90, and the spreads rise to the south: sd 2, 6, 12 and 25 by row.\nheat &lt;- expand.grid(x = 1:6, row = 1:4) |&gt;\n  mutate(mean = 30 + 12 * (x - 1), sd = c(2, 6, 12, 25)[row], y = -row)\nheat_fill &lt;- scale_fill_distiller(\n  palette = \"Blues\", direction = 1, limits = c(30, 90),\n  oob = scales::squish, guide = \"none\"\n)\nheat_grid &lt;- geom_tile(\n  data = heat, aes(x, y), fill = NA, colour = \"white\", linewidth = 1.2\n)\nheat_theme &lt;- list(coord_fixed(expand = FALSE), theme_void())</code></pre></details>\n<figure>\n<div class=\"gr-grid\">\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>a. Subdivided tiles</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdibbler, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>spread({ by: \"draw\" })</code> inside each tile · n = 16</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-heat-subdivide.png\" alt=\"A grid of tiles, each cut into 16 vertical strips; the southern tiles have strips of very different shades.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(heat, { n: 16 }), {\n  color: Color.gradient([\"#f7fbff\", \"#6baed6\", \"#08306b\"]),\n})\n  .flow(\n    table({ by: { x: \"x\", y: \"row\" }, spacing: 2 }),\n    spread({ by: \"draw\", dir: \"x\", spacing: 0 }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ fill: value }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">set.seed(1980)\np &lt;- heat |&gt;\n  mutate(value = dist_normal(mean, sd)) |&gt;\n  ggplot(aes(x, y, fill = value)) +\n  geom_tile_sample(times = 16) +\n  heat_grid +\n  heat_fill +\n  heat_theme\nsave(p, \"heat-subdivide\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>b. Subdivided tiles in order of level</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>spread({ by: \"level\" })</code> inside each tile · n = 16</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-heat-sorted.png\" alt=\"A grid of tiles, each a ramp of 16 strips from pale on the left to dark on the right; the southern ramps run further.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(sample(heat, { n: 16 }), {\n  color: Color.gradient([\"#f7fbff\", \"#6baed6\", \"#08306b\"]),\n})\n  .flow(\n    table({ by: { x: \"x\", y: \"row\" }, spacing: 2 }),\n    spread({ by: \"level\", dir: \"x\", spacing: 0 }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ fill: value }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># The same 16 draws per tile, sorted and laid left to right.\nset.seed(1980)\nstrips &lt;- heat |&gt;\n  slice(rep(1:n(), each = 16)) |&gt;\n  mutate(value = rnorm(n(), mean, sd)) |&gt;\n  group_by(x, y) |&gt;\n  arrange(value, .by_group = TRUE) |&gt;\n  mutate(i = row_number() - 1) |&gt;\n  ungroup()\np &lt;- ggplot(strips) +\n  geom_rect(aes(\n    xmin = x - 0.5 + i / 16, xmax = x - 0.5 + (i + 1) / 16,\n    ymin = y - 0.5, ymax = y + 0.5, fill = value\n  )) +\n  heat_grid +\n  heat_fill +\n  heat_theme\nsave(p, \"heat-sorted\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>c. The average picture, beside the color of the mean</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" })</code> · n = Infinity (top); the mean of the draws (bottom)</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-heat-blend.png\" alt=\"Two grids of flat tiles. In the top grid the southern row is paler on the east and darker on the west than in the bottom grid.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nconst color = Color.gradient([\n  \"#f7fbff\",\n  \"#6baed6\",\n  \"#08306b\",\n]);\n// top: the average of the draws' colors\nconst blend = chart(sample(heat, { n: Infinity }), {\n  color,\n})\n  .flow(\n    table({ by: { x: \"x\", y: \"row\" }, spacing: 2 }),\n    group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(rect({ fill: value }));\n// bottom: the color of the mean of the draws\nconst mean = chart(heat, { color })\n  .flow(table({ by: { x: \"x\", y: \"row\" }, spacing: 2 }))\n  .mark(rect({ fill: value.mean() }));\nspread({ dir: \"y\", spacing: 16 }, [blend, mean]);</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># Left: the limit of the blend, the average (in sRGB) of the colors of 4000\n# draws per tile. Right: the color of each tile's mean value.\nset.seed(1980)\navg_color &lt;- function(mean, sd) {\n  rgb_draws &lt;- col2rgb(heat_fill$map(rnorm(4000, mean, sd)))\n  rgb(t(rowMeans(rgb_draws)), maxColorValue = 255)\n}\npanels &lt;- bind_rows(\n  heat |&gt; mutate(\n    panel = \"average of the draws' colors\",\n    color = mapply(avg_color, mean, sd)\n  ),\n  heat |&gt; mutate(\n    panel = \"color of the mean value\",\n    color = heat_fill$map(mean)\n  )\n) |&gt; mutate(panel = factor(panel, levels = unique(panel)))\np &lt;- ggplot(panels, aes(x, y)) +\n  geom_tile(aes(fill = I(color)), colour = \"white\", linewidth = 1.2) +\n  facet_wrap(~panel, ncol = 1) +\n  heat_theme +\n  theme(strip.text = element_text(size = 10, margin = margin(2, 0, 4, 0)))\nsave(p, \"heat-blend\", height = 4.4)</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>d. A summary per tile</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: the median and the 95% central interval of each tile's draws · exact, no <code>sample</code></p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-heat-summary.png\" alt=\"A grid of flat tiles, each with a black bar along its bottom; the bars grow longer to the south.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const value = dist.normal({ mean: \"mean\", sd: \"sd\" });\nchart(heat, {\n  color: Color.gradient([\"#f7fbff\", \"#6baed6\", \"#08306b\"]),\n})\n  .flow(table({ by: { x: \"x\", y: \"row\" }, spacing: 2 }))\n  .mark(\n    layer([\n      // fill: the median of the draws\n      rect({ fill: value.median() }),\n      // a bar along the bottom: its length is the size of\n      // the 95% central interval of the draws\n      chart()\n        .flow(\n          derive((rows) =&gt;\n            centralIntervals(rows, value, [0.95]),\n          ),\n        )\n        .mark(rect({ w: \"size\", h: 4, fill: \"black\" })),\n    ]),\n  );</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># Fill: the median. The black bar along each tile's bottom is the width of\n# the 95% interval, on one scale for all tiles: the widest fills the tile.\nwidths &lt;- heat |&gt;\n  mutate(\n    width = qnorm(0.975, mean, sd) - qnorm(0.025, mean, sd),\n    bar = 0.9 * width / max(width)\n  )\np &lt;- ggplot(widths) +\n  geom_tile(aes(x, y, fill = qnorm(0.5, mean, sd))) +\n  geom_rect(aes(\n    xmin = x - 0.45, xmax = x - 0.45 + bar,\n    ymin = y - 0.42, ymax = y - 0.3\n  ), fill = \"black\") +\n  heat_grid +\n  heat_fill +\n  heat_theme\nsave(p, \"heat-summary\")</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n</div>\n<figcaption><p>Cards a and b carry the spread. In the southern row a tile holds pale and dark strips side by side, and in card b the spread shows as how far each ramp runs from pale to dark. Card b draws its own 16 values per tile with the same seed, so its strips are not exactly card a's draws.</p><p>As n grows, the strips get thinner, because a tile has a fixed area. So more draws and smaller strips are one limit, not two. The order of the strips decides what that limit is. In card a, the strips never settle: each n gives fresh noise. Once they are thinner than the eye can resolve, the tile looks like its average color, which is card c. In card b, every point in the tile settles on the color of one quantile, so the tile becomes a smooth ramp that still shows the spread.</p>\n<p>The limit of card b's sorted strips is a smooth color ramp inside each tile. Drawing it needs a color that varies along one region, the same GoFish gap as the gradient interval, so that limit cannot be written today.</p>\n<p>Card c draws two pictures. The top grid is the blend at the limit: each tile's color is the average of its draws' colors. The bottom grid is the color of each tile's mean value. They differ where the palette bends or clips. In the southern row many draws fall outside 30 to 90 and are drawn at the palette's ends, so the average color moves toward the middle of the palette: the eastern tiles turn paler and the western tiles darker than the color of their means. This is the gap between the average of a function and the function of the average. The blend shows the average color, not the color of the mean.</p>\n<p>Card d shows the median as the fill and the size of the 95% interval as the length of a bar along each tile's bottom. A bar is read by its length, which is more exact than a second color, and it leaves the fill to the median. All bars share one scale, so the widest interval fills its tile. For a normal the median is the mean, so card d's fill matches the bottom grid of card c.</p>\n<p>No rule fires on these cards. A tile grid joins no cells into one shape, so rule 3.2 has nothing to check, and nothing is stacked, so rule 3.4 does not apply. Card c uses n = Infinity with a mean blend, and card d uses summaries, which rule 3.1 allows. At n = Infinity, cards a and b would both be errors under rule 3.1, because each places one strip per draw. Card b's strips do tend to a ramp as n grows, but that limit is a second spec, which needs the gap named above.</p></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>5.6 Two levels: the blurred dotplot</h3>\n<p>Forty estimates, each with its own standard error. Each estimate is exp(−0.3 + 0.6 z) with z standard normal, and each standard error is uniform between 0.03 and 0.2 (seed 1980). This is the data of Figure 1 in <a href=\"#limits\">Blur as a limit</a>, drawn again in R so that card a is ggdist's own chart, not a copy of it. Bins are 0.2 wide in both cards.</p>\n<details><summary>R setup for this section</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># 40 estimates, each with its own standard error, as in \"Blur as a limit\".\nset.seed(1980)\nestimates &lt;- data.frame(\n  estimate = exp(-0.3 + 0.6 * rnorm(40)),\n  se = runif(40, 0.03, 0.2)\n)\nblur_x &lt;- list(\n  scale_x_continuous(limits = c(0, 4), expand = c(0, 0)),\n  labs(x = \"estimate\")\n)\nblur_theme &lt;- theme(\n  axis.text.y = element_blank(), axis.title.y = element_blank(),\n  panel.grid.major.y = element_blank()\n)</code></pre></details>\n<figure>\n<div class=\"gr-grid\">\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>a. ggdist's blurred dotplot</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge real\">ggdist, real render</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">No draws: each dot is binned and stacked by its estimate, then blurred sideways by its standard error</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-blur-ggdist.png\" alt=\"A dotplot whose dots are smeared sideways, with high dots smeared over empty space.\">\n  \n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">// Not writable: no mark has a blur option. The nearest\n// spelling, rejected in \"Blur as a limit\":\nchart(estimates)\n  .flow(\n    partition({\n      by: field(\"estimate\").bin(Bin.step(0.2)),\n      dir: \"x\",\n    }),\n    stack({ dir: \"y\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(circle({ r: 5, blur: \"se\" }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\">p &lt;- ggplot(estimates, aes(x = estimate, sd = se)) +\n  geom_blur_dots(binwidth = 0.2, fill = FILL, color = NA, overflow = \"keep\") +\n  blur_x +\n  blur_theme\nsave(p, \"blur-ggdist\", height = 3.4)</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n<div class=\"gr-card\">\n  <h4>b. Dotplot per draw, blended</h4>\n  <p class=\"gr-badge emu\">ggplot2 emulation of a grammar chart: in neither package</p>\n  <p class=\"gr-cell\">Uses up the draws: <code>group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" })</code>, then <code>partition</code> by a bin and <code>stack</code> inside each draw · n = 200</p>\n  <img src=\"img/gr-blur-twolevel.png\" alt=\"A dotplot whose stacks fade upward as well as sideways.\">\n  <p class=\"gr-note\">Each draw is binned and stacked with ggdist's own <code>bin_dots()</code>, so the layout matches card a. The blend is an exact mean over a raster.</p>\n  <pre><code class=\"language-js\">const x = dist.normal({ mean: \"estimate\", sd: \"se\" });\nchart(sample(estimates, { n: 200 }))\n  .flow(\n    group({ by: \"draw\", blend: \"mean\" }),\n    partition({ by: x.bin(Bin.step(0.2)), dir: \"x\" }),\n    stack({ dir: \"y\" }),\n  )\n  .mark(circle({ r: 5 }));</code></pre>\n  <details><summary>R code</summary><pre><code class=\"language-r\"># For each of 200 outer draws, draw every estimate from Normal(estimate, se),\n# bin and stack that draw's dots with ggdist's own bin_dots(), and add each\n# dot's coverage to a raster with weight 1/200 (a mean blend, not \"over\").\nset.seed(1980)\nn_outer &lt;- 200\nr &lt;- 0.1 # dot radius: half the 0.2 bin\npx &lt;- 0.005 # raster cell size, in data units\ngrid_x &lt;- seq(px / 2, 4, by = px)\ngrid_y &lt;- seq(px / 2, 3, by = px)\ncoverage &lt;- matrix(0, length(grid_y), length(grid_x))\nfor (d in seq_len(n_outer)) {\n  x &lt;- pmax(0, rnorm(nrow(estimates), estimates$estimate, estimates$se))\n  dots &lt;- bin_dots(x, 0, binwidth = 0.2)\n  for (k in seq_len(nrow(dots))) {\n    ix &lt;- which(abs(grid_x - dots$x[k]) &lt;= r)\n    iy &lt;- which(abs(grid_y - dots$y[k] - r) &lt;= r)\n    inside &lt;- outer(grid_y[iy] - dots$y[k] - r, grid_x[ix] - dots$x[k],\n      function(dy, dx) dx^2 + dy^2 &lt;= r^2)\n    coverage[iy, ix] &lt;- coverage[iy, ix] + inside / n_outer\n  }\n}\nraster &lt;- expand.grid(y = grid_y, x = grid_x)\nraster$coverage &lt;- as.vector(coverage)\np &lt;- ggplot(filter(raster, coverage &gt; 0), aes(x, y, alpha = coverage)) +\n  geom_raster(fill = FILL) +\n  scale_alpha_identity() +\n  coord_fixed(ylim = c(0, 3), expand = FALSE) +\n  blur_x +\n  blur_theme\nsave(p, \"blur-twolevel\", height = 3.4)</code></pre></details>\n</div>\n</div>\n<figcaption><p>Compare the tops of the stacks. In card a, the two highest dots of the tallest stack, near 0.5, are smeared from about 0.1 to 0.9, over places where no stack is that tall. That is the cliff from ggdibbler paper Figure 14. In card b, a dot that could land in a neighboring bin also lands at a different height there, so the stacks fade upward as well as sideways, and nothing is drawn above a bin unless some draw stacks that high. Card b's stacks also reach higher than card a's, because some draws pile more values into one bin than the point estimates do.</p>\n<p>Card b stacks draws of different estimates inside one draw, which are parts of one picture, so rule 3.4 does not apply. Here each estimate has a known standard error. ggdist's MCSE dots (Catalog entries 65 and 66) have only a sample, so they would need a Dist of Dists made from it, and that does not fit version 2 (see the Catalog).</p></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h2>6. Open questions</h2>\n<p>For Josh:</p>\n<ol type=\"a\">\n  <li><code>level</code> is a column that <code>sample</code> adds. Should it stay a column, or be computed on demand from the draw's rank inside its cell? As a column it is simple to read. On demand it would also work for data that arrives as draws, where the cell is not written down (Catalog, ggdist 16 and 21).</li>\n  <li>Should <code>sample</code> be only a data step, or also a flow step when no layers share it? As a flow step, the specs in 5.1 could split by category first and draw after, as version 1 did. As only a data step there is one place it can go, which is simpler.</li>\n  <li>Summaries are general, not special to draws. Today GoFish has <code>count</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>mean</code> and <code>median</code> on a field. <code>quantile(p)</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>max</code>, the probability of a bin, the density and the CDF are new. The density and the CDF return a curve, not one number. Which of these should exist, and how does a channel read a curve?</li>\n  <li>Should rules 3.2 and 3.3 warn or error? The Catalog gives 13 ggdibbler charts a 3.2 warning and one a 3.3 warning. All 14 are charts ggdibbler draws on purpose.</li>\n  <li>Does ggdist's \"second kind\" of mark need its own name? These are the gradient intervals and the fades, where a summary of the draws sets opacity along a region. They are seven Catalog entries, all marked as a GoFish gap.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For Harriet:</p>\n<ol type=\"a\" start=\"6\">\n  <li>Does the table in section 2 miss any ggdibbler position or ggdist geom you know of? The Catalog checks every example in both packages, but not every option.</li>\n</ol>\n";
